<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="allantoin degradation to ureidoglycolate I (urea producing)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5697" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Many organisms that degrade |FRAME: S-ALLANTOIN| do so with the concomitant production of |FRAME: UREA|. There are two steps in which urea can form during allantoin degradation, and the first of them is the conversion of |FRAME: ALLANTOATE| to |FRAME: CPD-1091| (other organisms can perform this conversion with production of ammonia, see |FRAME: PWY-5698|). The enzyme responsible for this reaction is |FRAME:CPLX-7519|, also known as allantoate amidinohydrolase. The |FRAME: UREA| that is formed is usually broken down to ammonia, either directly, by the enzyme |FRAME: CPLX-6897|, or in two steps, via the intermediate |FRAME: CPD-578| (see |FRAME:Urea-Degradation|).</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="xue" />
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
